A||||5||Bruce||exhaust tubing||||
Z||000000||Bruce||09-06-99||09:58 AM||||DXR_DAD and I are wondering about replacing the stock exhaust tubing with 2 1/4" to <br>2 1/2" diameter. Is mandrel-bending recommended, or does it increase flow and decrease low end torque? I'm replacing my cat. converter with a high flow type and also adding a Flowmaster muffler. I'ld like to keep the stock exhaust tips for "look and feel."<br>Over to you guys.
Z||000001||Shaun41178||09-06-99||10:44 AM||||Well I thought that I needed mandrel bent exhaust piping as well.  I went to a local muffler shop type place and this guy raced an old mustang in his spare time on road courses and stuff.  I told him I wanted mandrel bends and he said it wouldn't give much more power if any in my application which is 2.5 inch diamter piping with the flowmaster.  he said that with the small displacement engine we have a 2.5 inch will flow a lot better then the stock 2 inch even if it is not mandrel bent.  So I trust what the guy is talking about and it does make a little sense too.  <p>This setup of 2.5 inches will increase flow and probably decrease low end torque as well but will probably give a few ponies in the top end.  Hope this helps.<p>I have yet to get the muffler and stuff put in yet though.  Talk about me being lazy.  Oh yea the cost of the job should be around $135 or so.  That is what I was quoted.  Hopefully you can get it done for less.
Z||000002||Bruce||09-06-99||04:03 PM||||Thanks- that makes sense. I've heard that the exhaust tips can maintain enough back pressure to hold low end torque, despite the increase flow. Again, any advise about good sounding and reliable exhaust tips?
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||09-06-99||10:09 PM||||Thanks Shaun, this is what I thought, it makes sense. The price for some of these systems is a rip-off, and not needed. The 2 1/2 piping should make up for the crimp bend as far as flow.<br>Bruce I also think it can be done for a lot less than shaun`s price and we live in high price city?? let me know what ya gonna do
Z||000004||Shaun41178||09-08-1999||09:12 AM||||Bruce i guess it depends on what your tastes are as far as tips go and how much you want to spend.  The stock tips will sound good and a muffler shop should be able to weld the stock tips onto a 2.5 inch pipe or get them to mount someway.  If you want to spend some money or have money to spend, Borla make ssome nice tips.  They make dual tips of 2.5 inch diameter which would go well togethor with a 2.5 inch exhasut system.  These Borla tips like the square and round ones can be expensive but they have a nice sound to them and look good too.  You can get them from Jegs or summit or you might be able to see pics of them if Borla hasa  website.<P>I plan on going with Borla 2.5 inch dual angle round for my setup.  These price in the range of around $65 A piece I think and I will need to buy two of them of course.  But man will it look good and sound nice too.||||reg||
Z||000005||WisconsinGT||09-08-1999||05:47 PM||||I have the borla tips. They are great. The car sounds really good. It is not loud, but has a nice roar to it. ||||reg||
